#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Refuse commits in the SHARED dev checkout. Work belongs in a per-task worktree.
#
# WHY: ~/projects/moprox-tooling is shared by three interactive sessions and five agents. Twice now a
# session left it on a feature branch, another committed into it believing it was main, and
# `git push origin main` reported "Everything up-to-date" while the work sat stranded — because that
# pushes the local `main` REF, not your HEAD. One of those strandings hid services/lib/errlog.py for a
# day while a module on main imported it.
#
# Worktrees fix this structurally: own HEAD, own branch, shares the object store (so it is nearly
# free), and lives minutes-to-hours so it cannot diverge. This hook only blocks the shared tree —
# worktrees have a different toplevel and commit normally.
SHARED="/home/mikael/projects/moprox-tooling"
TOP="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)"
[ "$TOP" != "$SHARED" ] && exit 0
[ -n "${MOPROX_ALLOW_SHARED_COMMIT:-}" ] && exit 0

cat >&2 <<'MSG'
REFUSED: this is the shared dev checkout — commit from a worktree instead.

    cd ~/projects/moprox-tooling
    git fetch origin
    git worktree add -b <task-name> /tmp/<task-name> origin/main
    cd /tmp/<task-name>          # work, commit here
    git push origin HEAD:main    # HEAD, never `main` — see below
    cd - && git worktree remove /tmp/<task-name>

Push `HEAD:main`, not `main`: `git push origin main` pushes the local main ref, which is
usually unchanged, so it prints "Everything up-to-date" and silently discards your work.

Genuinely need this one commit here?  MOPROX_ALLOW_SHARED_COMMIT=1 git commit ...
MSG
exit 1
